   never be padded to a tall one. That padding was the 310px void. --- */
.grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:3fr 2fr; gap:var(--gap-3);
        align-items:start; margin-top:var(--gap-3); }
.cell { min-width:0; }              /* lets children shrink instead of overflow */
.cell.wide { grid-column:1 / -1; }
@media (max-width:1000px) { .grid { grid-template-columns:1fr; } }

/* --- fit the window, whatever the tree does (issue #56) ---
   The page height was a function of how much work exists: it went 1,721 ->
   1,792px in half an hour purely because worktrees were added. Cutting lists
   to fit only moves that number, it does not stop it moving — measured, even
   deleting the whole pipeline left 1,196px against a 900px budget, because
   work packages and handovers set the floor. (Handovers came off in #64,
   which is why there is headroom now — the cap stays: the point was never
   those particular regions, it was that height tracked the size of the tree.)
   So the regions are capped and scroll inside themselves instead. The page
   fits by construction and stops tracking the size of the tree. Nothing is
   dropped and nothing is hidden behind a disclosure.
   Only above the single-column breakpoint: a narrow window is a browser being
   read, not the wall screen, and locking that to the viewport would squash it. */
@media (min-width:1001px) {
  html, body { height:100%; }
  body { overflow:hidden; }
  .tower { height:100%; display:flex; flex-direction:column; }
  /* The band is the most important thing on the screen and still must not own
     it: unbounded, three waiting items took 28% of the height and squeezed the
     pipeline to 165px. Capped, it scrolls like everything else. */
  .band { flex:0 0 auto; max-height:22vh; overflow-y:auto; }
  .grid { flex:1 1 auto; min-height:0;
          /* Two rows now, not three: #64 took handovers and the ticker off, and
             a template still declaring their row left the sessions strip in a
             1fr track it does not need (211px for 123px of dots) plus an empty
             third track. The work takes everything left over; the strip sizes
             to its content. */
          grid-template-rows:minmax(0,1fr) auto;
          /* stretch, not start: with align-items:start a grid item sizes to
             its content and simply overflows its track, so the cap never
             binds and the overflow lands back on the page. */
          align-items:stretch; }
  /* min-height:0 is the load-bearing half — without it a grid item refuses to
     shrink below its content and the overflow moves back onto the page. */
  .grid > .cell { overflow-y:auto; min-height:0; }
  .grid > .cell.wide { overflow:hidden; }   /* the strip row sizes to content */
  .foot { flex:0 0 auto; margin-top:var(--gap-2); }
